      overview: "Discover how rocks transform from one type to another through geological processes over millions of years.",
      mainContent: `## Nothing Is Permanent

Rocks seem eternal and unchanging. In human timescales, they are. But over geological time, rocks transform continuously through the rock cycle — a system that recycles Earth's crust through heat, pressure, weathering, and melting.

**Igneous rocks** form when molten magma or lava cools and solidifies. Granite, formed from slowly cooling magma deep underground, has large crystals because atoms had time to organize into crystal structures. Basalt, formed from rapidly cooling lava at Earth's surface, has tiny crystals. The ocean floor is predominantly basalt, created at mid-ocean ridges where tectonic plates diverge.

**Sedimentary rocks** form from accumulated sediments — fragments of other rocks, minerals, or organic material. Rivers carry sediment to ocean basins, where it accumulates in layers. Over millions of years, burial compacts the sediment and minerals precipitate between grains, cementing them into solid rock. Limestone forms from accumulated shells of marine organisms. Sandstone forms from ancient beaches and deserts.

**Metamorphic rocks** form when existing rocks are subjected to intense heat and pressure without melting. Limestone transforms into marble. Shale becomes slate, then schist, then gneiss as pressure increases. The atoms rearrange into new crystal structures stable at higher temperatures and pressures. Metamorphic rocks often form in mountain-building zones where tectonic forces compress and heat the crust.

**The cycle has no beginning or end.** Igneous rocks weather into sediment, which becomes sedimentary rock, which may be metamorphosed, which may melt into magma, which crystallizes into new igneous rock. Or the cycle may skip steps: sedimentary rock can melt directly into magma, or igneous rock can be metamorphosed without first becoming sediment.

> The sand on a beach represents rocks in mid-cycle. Those quartz grains may have been part of granite in a mountain range, weathered free over millions of years, and will eventually compact into sandstone, completing their transformation.

**James Hutton**, the 18th-century Scottish geologist, recognized that geological processes operating today have worked throughout Earth's history. He observed layers of sedimentary rock tilted vertically, eroded, and buried under horizontal layers — evidence of multiple cycles of deposition, uplift, erosion, and reburial. He called this "deep time," realizing Earth must be vastly older than anyone imagined.`,
